# 
# Help File Format Information
# 
# Special characters at the beginning of each line determine
# the function of that line:
#       `#' = Comment line, ignored by program
#       `:' = Control line. the program looks for these
#             lines, and reads the following characters as a
#             help subject label. One special case is
#             ":page", which indicates a page break within a
#             help subject. 
# Maximum line length for help items is 61 chrs. Longer lines
# are truncated. Maximum number of lines/screen is 16
# (additional lines are automatically moved to the next
# screen).
# 
#-----------------------------------------------------------
#
:chedit_chno

      [Channel Number for Display on the Transceiver]

 Pressing the Space Bar toggles the data for the entire
 channel between hidden and unhidden (except the first
 channel, which cannot be hidden).

 Hidden channels display "--" in place of field entries, and
 are not used for operation (although they are still stored
 in hidden form for possible recall later).

 Channel numbers cannot be edited.

 Space Bar:  Hide/Unhide entire channel (line).
#
#
#
:chedit_rxf
   [Edit Rx/Simplex Frequency & Hide/Unhide Channel Data]

 Use 0 - 9 to enter the desired channel frequency, and press
 Enter. This frequency will be adjusted if it does not match
 the Channel Step parameter, and will also appear in the Tx
 Freq field. You do not need to enter all 8 digits: empty
 digits to the right are zero-filled. Pressing [.] (period)
 after several digits forces them to MHz. Press period first
 to change only kHz.

 Pressing only the Space Bar without entering a number
 toggles the data for the entire channel between hidden and
 unhidden (except the first channel, which cannot be hidden).
 Hidden channels display "--" in place of field entries, and
 are not used for operation (although they are still stored
 in hidden form for possible recall later).
#
:page
#

 NOTE! Any entries or changes to this field will also occur
       in the Tx Freq. field, so you may need to edit that
       field after making any changes here.


 Arrow Keys: Change field (before or after editing 
                Frequency).
 Space Bar:  Hide/Unhide entire channel (line).
 0 - 9:      Input a digit.
 [.]:        Set last three (or fewer) digits as MHz, or
                next digits as kHz (if pressed first).
 Backspace:  Erase digit to the left, or cancel entry.
 Enter Key:  Accept displayed frequency, with zero-fill.
 Esc Key:    Cancel and return to previous frequency.

#
#
#
:chedit_txf

  [Enter Transmit Frequency & Enable/Disable Transmission]

 Use keys 0 - 9 to enter the desired transmitting frequency,
 and press the Enter key to accept. If the frequency does 
 not match the Channel Step parameter, it will be adjusted 
 to the nearest valid step. You do not need to enter all 8 
 digits.  Empty digits to the right are zero-filled.
 Pressing [.] (period) after several digits forces them to
 MHz, while pressing [.] first allows entering only kHz.
 
 Pressing the Space Bar without entering a number toggles
 the Tx Inhibit to On, and disables all of the remaining
 transmitter-specific items.
#
:page
#

 Arrow Keys: Change field (before or after editing 
                Frequency).
 Space Bar:  Toggle Tx Inhibit
 0 - 9:      Input a digit.
 [.]:        Set last three (or fewer) digits as MHz, or
                next digits as kHz (if pressed first).
 Backspace:  Erase digit to the left, or cancel entry.
 Enter Key:  Accept displayed frequency, with zero-fill.
 Esc Key:    Cancel and return to previous frequency.
#
#
#
:chedit_cdec

 [Toggle Subaudible CTCSS Decoder, and set CTCSS frequency]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the decoder on/off, or press
 Enter to display the Tone Select window, from which you may
 select a frequency with the Arrow Keys (pressing Enter again
 to accept, or Esc to cancel).

 If you know the CTCSS frequency you want, you can enter it
 directly with the 0 - 9 and [.] (period) keys. When the
 desired CTCSS frequency is displayed, press Enter to accept.
 If the frequency was invalid (not among the standard tones),
 the Tone Select Window will appear as described above, with
 the nearest valid frequency pre-selected.
#
:page
#

 47 CTCSS tones, including eight non-standard tones, are
 available for programming.

 Turning on CTCSS turns off DCS (both cannot be on).


 Arrow Keys: Change field, or select in Tone Select window.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Decoder on/off (except during selection).
 0 - 9, [.]: Direct tone frequency entry.
 Backspace:  Delete digit to left during direct entry.
 Enter Key:  Display Tone Select Window, and accept entry/
               selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel input or selection.
#
#
#
#
:chedit_ddec

           [Toggle DCS Decoder, and set DCS Code]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the decoder on/off, or press
 Enter to display the Code Select window, from which you may
 select a DCS Code with the Arrow Keys (pressing Enter again
 to accept, or Esc to cancel).

 If you know the 3-digit DCS Code you want, you can enter it
 directly with the 0 - 9 keys. When the desired Code is
 displayed, press Enter to accept. If the Code was invalid
 (not among the standard Code numbers), the Code Select
 window will appear as described above, with the nearest
 valid Code pre-selected.
#
:page
#

 104 DCS codes are available for programming.

 Turning on DCS turns off CTCSS (both cannot be on).
 

 Arrow Keys: Change field, or select in Code Select window.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Decoder on/off (except during selection).
 0 - 7:      Direct Code number entry.
 Backspace:  Delete digit to left during direct entry.
 Enter Key:  Display Code Select Window, and accept entry/
               selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel input or selection.
#
#
#
:chedit_cenc

 [Toggle Subaudible CTCSS Encoder, and set CTCSS frequency]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the encoder on/off, or press
 Enter to display the Tone Select window, from which you may
 select a frequency with the Arrow Keys (pressing Enter 
 again to accept, or Esc to cancel).

 If you know the CTCSS frequency you want, you can enter it
 directly with the 0 - 9 and [.] (period) keys. When the
 desired CTCSS frequency is displayed, press Enter to accept.
 If the frequency was invalid (not among the standard tones),
 the Tone Select Window will appear as described above, with
 the nearest valid frequency pre-selected.
#
:page
#

 47 CTCSS tones, including eight non-standard tones, are
 available for programming.

 Turning on CTCSS turns off DCS (both cannot be on).
 
 Arrow Keys: Change field, or select in Tone Select window.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Encoder on/off (except during selection).
 0 - 9, [.]: Direct tone frequency entry.
 Backspace:  Delete digit to left during direct entry.
 Enter Key:  Display Tone Select Window, and accept entry/
               selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel input or selection.
#
#
#
:chedit_denc

            [Toggle DCS Encoder, and set DCS Code]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the encoder on/off, or press
 Enter to display the Code Select window, from which you may
 select a DCS Code with the Arrow Keys (pressing Enter again
 to accept, or Esc to cancel).

 If you know the 3-digit DCS Code you want, you can enter it
 directly with the 0 - 9 keys. When the desired Code is
 displayed, press Enter to accept. If the Code was invalid
 (not among the standard Code numbers), the Code Select
 window will appear as described above, with the nearest
 valid Code pre-selected.
#
:page
#

 104 DCS codes are available for programming.

 Turning on DCS turns off CTCSS (both cannot be on).
 

 Arrow Keys: Change field, or select in Code Select window.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Encoder on/off (except during selection).
 0 - 7:      Direct Code number entry.
 Backspace:  Delete digit to left during direct entry.
 Enter Key:  Display Code Select Window, and accept entry/
               selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel input or selection.
#
#
#
:chedit_wide

                   [Wide/Narrow toggle]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the FM mode Wide or Narrow.
 Wide = 25 kHz channel spacing, 5 kHz deviation.
 Narrow = 12.5 kHz channel spacing, 2.5 kHz deviation.
 Both the transmitter deviation and the receiver bandwidth
 are changed.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle wide/narrow.
 
#
#
#
:chedit_clksft

                     [CPU Clock Shift]

 The software automatically assigns clock shift status as 
 may be needed for the programmed frequency.  If a 
 noticeable buzz is heard on the signal (Tx or Rx), 
 changing the status of the CPU Clock Shift will usually 
 cure it.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle on/off.

#
#
#
:chedit_nsqmd

                   [Noise Squelch Mode]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the repeater noise squelch 
 selection mode between Prpgm or User.

 "Prpgm" selects the factory's pre-set level, which is 
 about the 12dB SINAD point and is equal to User setting
 48. "User" lets the programmer change this threshold
 level on a per-channel basis. 

#
:page
#
 
 Note: The repeater's front panel Squelch knob only
 controls the squelch of the local receiver.  It does not
 adjust the internal repeater squelch threshold.

 
 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Prpgm (Pre Program)/User.

#
#
#
:chedit_nsqfxlv

                   [Noise Squelch Level]

 Press the Space Bar to increment, or the Backspace key
 to decrement, the Noise Squelch Level when the Noise 
 Squelch Mode is set to "User".  You may also enter
 numeric digits (0 - 9) followed by the Enter key.
 
 Numeric values of 0 to 255 are available.  Lower numbers
 set a "loose" squelch, higher numbers set a "tight"
 squelch.  Values over 100 usually result in saturation
 of the squelch threshold circuit.  Very little change is
 observed with values over 100.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar/Backspace: Increment/Decrement NSQ Level.
#
#
#
:chedit_cblip

                      [Courtesy Blip]

 When ON, the VXR-7000 sends out a Courtesy Beep each 
 time a user unkeys their radio.  The Beep indicates 
 that the Time-Out Timer has reset, and the next user
 may begin transmitting. 
 
 Arrow Keys: Change fields after setting on/off.
 Space Bar:  Toggle on/off.
#
#
#
:chedit_revbst

                      [Reverse Burst]


 Press the Space Bar to toggle the Reverse Burst on/off.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle on/off.

#
#
#
:chedit_dcstype

                        [DCS Type]

 Effective only when DCS is chosen as the coded squelch.
 A = Normal DCS
 B = Inverted (complement) DCS

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle A/B.
#
#
#
:chedit_ddectype

                       [DCS Dec Type]
  
 Selects ability to decode the DCS complement.
 Fixed = Decodes the selected type only (normal OR inverted)
 Auto  = Decodes both types (normal AND inverted)

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Auto/Fixed.
#
#
#
:chedit_mlttone

                   [Multi Tone Signaling]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the Multi Tone feature 
 on/off. If necessary, press the Enter key to bring up the 
 Multi Tone programming table.

 Use the Arrow keys, Enter key, and numeric digits to
 make entries into the Multi Tone table.  Table entries
 are made in the same manner as the single tone (code)
 entries made under CTCSS (DCS) Encode and Decode on the 
 Main Programming Screen. 
#
:page
#

 Up to 16 different Encode and Decode tones (CTCSS) or codes 
 (DCS) can be entered into the table. Tones or codes can be 
 mixed. For example, a decoded CTCSS tone of 100.0 Hz can 
 activate the repeater and cause it to transmit a DCS code 
 of 051 if desired.  The repeater will decode and repeat on
 any tone (code) programmed into the Decode column, and will
 transmit the one tone (code) programmed into the corresp-
 onding Encode column.  If a tone (code) is entered on the 
 Main Programming Screen, it will be used in the Tone 01
 position in the table.  

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle on/off.
 Enter Key:  Display Multi-Tone Select window.
 
#
#
#
:chedit_mtonesel
                    [Multi Tone Select]

 If you know the CTCSS frequency or DCS code you want, you 
 can enter it directly with the 0 - 9 and [.] (period) keys.
 When the display shows the desired CTCSS frequency or DCS 
 code, press Enter to accept.
 If the frequency was invalid (not among the standard tones)
 the Tone/Code Select Window will appear as mentioned above.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle on/off.
 0 - 9, [.]: Direct tone frequency entry.
 Backspace:  Delete digit to left during direct entry.
 Enter Key:  Display Tone Select Window, and accept entry/
               selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel input or selection.
#
#
#
:chedit_cwanisel

                  [CW-ID or ANI/ENI select]

 If CW-ID is desired, go to Common Data (F2) and set the CW 
 ID to On.  Also, program the CW-ID settings (F5) as
 desired.  Further Help is available for these items.

 If DTMF or 5 Tone signaling is desired, go to Common Data
 (F2) and set the DTMF ANI/ENI or 5 Tone ANI/ENI to the
 Enabled state as desired.  Also, program the DTMF or
 5 Tone settings (F4) as desired.  Further Help is
 available for these items.
#
:page
#

 Note: CWID and ANI/ENI cannot both be enabled on the same
 channel at the same time.

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the CW-ID or ANI/ENI to On.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle CW-ID, ANI/ENI and off.

#
#
#
:chedit_actmode

                       [Action Mode]


 Press the Space Bar to toggle the Action Mode between 
 Simplex/Duplex.

 Simplex is for fixed base or remote base use only.
 Duplex is for repeater operation.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Duplex/Simplex.

#
#
#@
:chedit_basetotuse

                      [Base Mode ToT]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the Base Mode ToT yes/no.

 When Yes, the Base Mode Time-out Timer is in use.  The
 Repeater must be in Base mode (front panel switch in the
 "BASE" position).  The Base ToT time period is set in
 the Common Data window.
 

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle yes/no.
#
#
#
:chedit_basegtuse

                     [Base Guard Timer]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the Base Guard Timer 
 yes/no.  When Yes, the Base Mode Guard Timer is in use.  
 The repeater must be in Base mode (front panel switch in 
 the "BASE" position).  The Base Guard time period is set 
 in the Common Data window.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle yes/no.
#
#
#
:chedit_lout

                 [Lock Out - BCLO and BTLO]

 The Space Bar toggles the transmitter Lock Out selection 
 between Carrier Control (BCLO), CTCSS/DCS Control (BTLO), 
 or off.  Lock Out is only in effect when the repeater is
 operating in the Base mode (front panel switch in the
 "BASE" position).

 Note: You must press Enter after making a selection.

 Arrow Keys: Change field (after pressing Enter).
 Space Bar:  Toggle BCLO mode.
#
#
#
:chedit_txpwrsel

                   [Transmit Power Select]

 Selects the Tx power level for this channel.  Choices 
 are High (normally 50 watts) and Low (10 watts).

 Arrow Keys:          Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar/Backspace: Increment/Decrement Transmit Power
                      Level.
#
#
#
:chedit_totmute

                      [ToT Beep Mute]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the ToT Beep Mute on/off.
 When On, the ToT warning beep will not be heard in the
 Repeater's local speaker.  The warning beep WILL still be
 heard by other radios on the air.

 Arrow Keys:          Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar/Backspace: Toggle on/off.
#
#
#
:chedit_rpttotuse

                     [Repeat ToT Use]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the Repeat ToT Use
 yes/no.  When Yes, the Repeater Mode Time-out Timer is
 active (front panel switch in the "REPEATER" position).
 The Repeater Time-out time period is set in the
 Common Data window.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle yes/no.
#
#
#
:chedit_rpttotbp

                     [Repeat ToT Beep]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the Repeat ToT Beep
 yes/no.  When Yes, the Time-out Timer warning beep
 will be tranmitted a few seconds prior to time-out.
 When No, the warning beep will not be transmitted.
 Of course, the Repeat ToT Use must be set to Yes for
 this to be active.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle yes/no.
#
#
#
:chedit_rpthtuse

                    [Repeat Hang Timer]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the Repeat Hang Timer 
 yes/no.  When Yes, the transmitter will remain on after
 a user unkeys for the amount of time programmed in the
 Common Data window under Repeat HangOn Time.  When No,
 the transmitter will drop immediately when a user unkeys
 his radio.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle yes/no.

#
#
#
:chedit_rptgtuse

                    [Repeat Guard Timer]

 Press the Space Bar to toggle the Repeat Guard Timer 
 yes/no.  When Yes, the Guard Time function is active.
 The Repeat Guard Time period is set in the Common Data
 window.  

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle yes/no.
#
#
#REM: The following 27 items appear in the Common Data Window
#
#
:comd_bandsel

                       [Band Select]

 Press the Space bar to toggle between VHF and UHF.  The
 new selection will take effect after the Esc. key is
 pressed to exit the Common Data window.  When the 
 opposite band range is selected, any previously programmed 
 channel data will be erased after a warning screen is 
 presented.  The correct IF frequency is automatically 
 selected for the band range.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle VHF/UHF.
#
#
#
:comd_dupxinst

                    [Duplexer Installed]

 This item only serves as a reminder to the programmer of
 the presence of the internal duplexer.  It does not have
 any other programming or operating effect.

 If the internal duplexer is installed, the repeater should
 only be programmed to transmit and receive on the exact
 frequencies to which the duplexer is aligned.

 Reminder:  If the VXD internal duplexer is installed, the
 transmitter power output must be reduced to not more than
 40 watts input to the duplexer.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle yes/no.
#
#
#
:comd_beepen

                       [Beep Enable]

 This item enables or disables the audible beeps for the 
 front panel control buttons.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle yes/no.
#
#
#
:comd_monen

                     [Monitor Enable]

 This item enables or disables the front panel Monitor
 button.  If enabled, pressing this button will temporarily
 place the repeater's receiver in carrier squelch mode.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle yes/no.
#
#
#
:comd_1stofst

                    [1st Local Offset]

 This item changes the 1st local oscillator offset between 
 Lower and Higher.

 DO NOT CHANGE THIS SETTING UNLESS YOU HAVE MODIFIED THE
 REPEATER'S HARDWARE APPROPRIATELY.  THIS IS AN ADVANCED
 PROCEDURE.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Lower/Upper.
#
#
#
:comd_2ndofst

                    [2nd Local Offset]

 This item changes the 2nd local oscillator offset between 
 Lower and Higher.

 DO NOT CHANGE THIS SETTING UNLESS YOU HAVE MODIFIED THE
 REPEATER'S HARDWARE APPROPRIATELY.  THIS IS AN ADVANCED
 PROCEDURE.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Lower/Upper.
#
#
#
:comd_accy

                        [Accessory]

 This item selects the function of the repeater's Accessory
 Button.  Two choices are available:  High/Low transmit 
 Power, or Acc.

 High/Low power is self-explanatory.  When set to Acc, the
 button switches the logic state of pin 21 on the ACCY
 connector on the rear of the repeater.  This is available
 to provide logic switching for activation of whatever
 device may be connected to pin 21.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle High/Low or Acc
#
#
#
:comd_micmoni

                   [Mic Monitor Enable]

 This item enables or disables the monitor function of the
 optional desktop microphone (if one is used).  If enabled,
 Pressing the microphone's monitor button will temporar-
 ily place the repeater's receiver in carrier squelch mode.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle yes/no.
#
#
#
:comd_fanalrt

                    [Fan Alert Enable]

 This item enables or disables the fan stoppage alert.  
 When enabled, a warning beep will sound if the cooling fan
 Stops running (for example, if dirt or another obstruction 
 jams and stops the fan from turning).

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle yes/no.
#
#
#
:comd_tempalrt

                      [Hi-Temp Alert]

 This item enables or disables the high-temperature alert.
 When enabled, a warning beep will sound if the heatsink
 overheats for any reason (for example, if the cooling
 fan is unable to sufficiently cool the heatsink).

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle yes/no.
#
#
#
:comd_hngaudio

                      [Hang On Audio]

 This item selects the audio that will be heard after a
 user unkeys his radio and the repeater is transmitting
 during the hang time.  The "Noise" setting will allow
 unsquelched channel audio to be transmitted during the
 hang time.  The "Quiet" setting will allow only squelched
 audio, effectively silence in most cases, to be trans-
 mitted during the hang time.  Most users will want this
 set to "Quiet".

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle Noise/Quiet.
#
#
#
:comd_chstep

                       [Channel Step]

 This item selects the step size for the programmed TX
 and RX frequencies.  Frequencies must be divisible by at
 least one of the selected resolution numbers.  Any 
 entered frequency that is not divisible by one of these
 numbers will be rounded-off to the nearest valid
 frequency.

 Available choices are 2.5/6.25 kHz or 5/6.25 kHz for VHF, 
 and 5/6.25 kHz (only) for UHF.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle 2.5/6.25 or 5/6.25 (VHF only)
#
#
#
:comd_comport

                         [Com Port]

 This item selects the serial port for computer programming
 to the repeater.  COM1 or COM2 are supported.

 Reminder: PCs running the Windows operating system must
 have Windows SHUT DOWN and the PC restarted in the MS DOS
 mode in order to successfully communicate with the
 repeater.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle COM1/COM2.
#
#
#
:comd_if

                           [IF]

 This item displays the Intermediate Frequency in use for 
 the selected band range.  The VHF IF is 21.40 MHz.  The 
 UHF IF is 73.35 MHz.

 It is possible to edit these selections by using the 
 number keys.  Be aware that an incorrect IF setting will
 result in no receiver operation.

 DO NOT CHANGE THIS SETTING UNLESS YOU HAVE MODIFIED THE
 REPEATER'S HARDWARE APPROPRIATELY.  THIS IS AN ADVANCED
 PROCEDURE.
#
:page
#

 Arrow Keys: Change field (before or after editing 
                frequency).
 0 - 9:      Input a digit.
 [.]:        Set last three (or fewer) digits as MHz, or
                next digits as kHz (if pressed first).
 Backspace:  Erase digit to the left, or cancel entry.
 Enter Key:  Accept displayed frequency, with zero-fill.
 Esc Key:    Cancel and return to previous frequency.
#
#
#
:comd_rxref

                      [RX Reference]

 This item sets the receiver's reference frequency.  The
 RX reference is 14.40 MHz for both VHF and UHF repeaters.

 It is possible to edit these selections by using the 
 number keys.  Be aware that an incorrect RX reference 
 setting will result in no receiver operation.

 DO NOT CHANGE THIS SETTING UNLESS YOU HAVE MODIFIED THE
 REPEATER'S HARDWARE APPROPRIATELY.  THIS IS AN ADVANCED
 PROCEDURE.
#
:page
#

 Arrow Keys: Change field (before or after editing 
                frequency).
 0 - 9:      Input a digit.
 [.]:        Set last three (or fewer) digits as MHz, or
                next digits as kHz (if pressed first).
 Backspace:  Erase digit to the left, or cancel entry.
 Enter Key:  Accept displayed frequency, with zero-fill.
 Esc Key:    Cancel and return to previous frequency.
#
#
#
:comd_txref

                      [TX Reference]

 This item sets the transmitter's reference frequency.  The
 TX reference is 14.40 MHz for both VHF and UHF repeaters.

 It is possible to edit these selections by using the 
 number keys.  Be aware that an incorrect TX reference 
 setting will result in no transmitter operation.

 DO NOT CHANGE THIS SETTING UNLESS YOU HAVE MODIFIED THE
 REPEATER'S HARDWARE APPROPRIATELY.  THIS IS AN ADVANCED
 PROCEDURE.
#
:page
#

 Arrow Keys: Change field (before or after editing 
                frequency).
 0 - 9:      Input a digit.
 [.]:        Set last three (or fewer) digits as MHz, or
                next digits as kHz (if pressed first).
 Backspace:  Erase digit to the left, or cancel entry.
 Enter Key:  Accept displayed frequency, with zero-fill.
 Esc Key:    Cancel and return to previous frequency.
#
#
#
:comd_txpwrtyp

                      [TX Power Type]

 This item selects the transmitter's high power setting.
 Toggle between 50 W (USA market) and 25 W (other markets).

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle 50W/25W.
#
#
#
:comd_dcpwrlo

                     [DC TX Power Low]

 This item selects whether the repeater will automatically
 switch to low transmit power (10 watts) when AC power is 
 lost and it switches to the backup battery for power.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle yes/no
#
#
#
:comd_hitmppwr

                    [Hi-Temp TX Pwr Low]

 This item selects whether the repeater will automatically
 switch to low transmit power (10 watts) when the heatsink
 becomes overheated.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle yes/no.
#
#
#
:comd_basetot

                        [Base ToT]

 This item programs the time period for the Base Mode Time-
 out Timer.  See Base ToT on the Main Programming Screen
 for the on/off selection for this feature.

 Use the number keys to program this value in 0.5 minute
 intervals from 0.5 through 60.0 minutes.

 Arrow Keys: Change field (before or after editing time).
 0 - 9:      Input a digit.
 [.]:        Input the decimal point.
 Backspace:  Erase digit to the left, or cancel entry.
 Enter Key:  Accept displayed time, with zero-fill.
 Esc Key:    Cancel and return to previous time.
#
#
#
:comd_basegd

                     [Base Guard Time]

 This item programs the time period for the Base Mode
 Guard Time.  See Base Guard on the Main Programming
 Screen for the on/off selection for this feature.

 Use the number keys to program this value in 2 second
 increments from 2 through 360 seconds.

 Arrow Keys: Change field (before or after editing time).
 0 - 9:      Input a digit.
 Backspace:  Erase digit to the left, or cancel entry.
 Enter Key:  Accept displayed time, with zero-fill.
 Esc Key:    Cancel and return to previous time.
#
#
#
:comd_rpttot

                       [Repeat ToT]

 This item programs the time period for the Repeater Mode
 Time-out Timer.  See RptToT on the Main Programming
 Screen for the on/off selection for this feature.

 Use the number keys to program this value in 0.5 minute
 increments from 0.5 through 60.0 minutes. 

 Arrow Keys: Change field (before or after editing time).
 0 - 9:      Input a digit.
 [.]:        Input the decimal point.
 Backspace:  Erase digit to the left, or cancel entry.
 Enter Key:  Accept displayed time, with zero-fill.
 Esc Key:    Cancel and return to previous time.
#
#
#
:comd_rpthngtm
                    [Repeat Hang On Time]

 This item programs the time period for the Repeater Hang
 Timer.  See RPT HT on the Main Programming Screen for the
 on/off selection for this feature.  Also, set the Hang 
 On audio to either Noise or Quiet as desired.

 Use the number keys to program this value in 0.5 second
 increments from 0.5 through 60.0 seconds.

 Arrow Keys: Change field (before or after editing time).
 0 - 9:      Input a digit.
 [.]:        Input the decimal point.
 Backspace:  Erase digit to the left, or cancel entry.
 Enter Key:  Accept displayed time, with zero-fill.
 Esc Key:    Cancel and return to previous time.
#
#
#
:comd_rptgrdtm

                    [Repeat Guard Time]

 This item programs the time period for the Repeater
 Guard Timer.  See RPT GT on the Main Programming Screen
 For the on/off selection for this feature.

 Use the number keys to program this value in 2 second
 increments from 2 through 360 seconds.

 Arrow Keys: Change field (before or after editing time).
 0 - 9:      Input a digit.
 [.]:        Input the decimal point.
 Backspace:  Erase digit to the left, or cancel entry.
 Enter Key:  Accept displayed time, with zero-fill.
 Esc Key:    Cancel and return to previous time.
#
#
#
:comd_cwid

                         [CW ID]

 This item toggles the CW ID (automatic Morse code 
 identifier) on/off.  When toggled on, the F5 key (see
 the red boxes at the bottom of the screen) allows access
 to the CW-ID Settings window, where the ID parameters
 can be programmed.  Also see CWID ANI/ENI on the Main
 Programming Screen to select the item that will be in 
 use on any particular channel.  CWID and ANI/ENI cannot
 both be active on any one channel.

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle on/off
#
#
#
:comd_dtmfa/e

                       [DTMF ANI/ENI]

 This item toggles the DTMF ANI/ENI feature between three
 On states (RX enable, TX enable, and TRX enable) and off.
 When RX, the repeater will only receive, decode and
 display incoming DTMF ANI/ENI signals.  When TX, the
 repeater will only transmit it's programmed DTMF ANI/ENI
 signals.  When TRX, both of the above are active.
#
:page
#

 When any of the On states are selected, the F4 key (see
 the red boxes at the bottom of the screen) allows access
 to the DTMF Settings window, where the ANI/ENI parameters
 can be programmed.  Note: The Mark and Space Time 
 parameters affect both the RX (decode) and TX (encode)
 operation.  Incoming ID strings from other radios must
 match the Mark and Space Time settings here to be 
 reliably decoded.

 DTMF ANI strings must always be 5 characters in length.  
 The first incoming character must be the ANI Header Code 
 character.
#
:page
#

 Alphanumeric displays for up to 48 incoming IDs can be
 programmed into the repeater.  The F6 key (see the red 
 boxes at the bottom of the screen) allows access to the
 Alpha Tag table for programming.  This table does not
 use the Header Code digit.  When an incoming (numeric) 
 ID matches an entry in this table, the programmed Alpha 
 Tag is displayed instead of the actual number.  If an 
 incoming ID is not in the table (but does have the 
 correct header character), the numeric ID is displayed.
#
:page
#

 Also see CWID ANI/ENI on the Main Programming Screen to 
 select the item that will be in use on any particular 
 channel.  CWID and ANI/ENI cannot both be active on any 
 one channel. 

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle RX Enable/TX Enable/TRX Enable/off
#
#
#
:comd_5tonea/e

                     [5-Tone ANI/ENI]

 This item toggles the 5 Tone ANI/ENI feature between three
 On states (RX enable, TX enable, and TRX enable) and off.
 When RX, the repeater will only receive, decode and
 display incoming 5 Tone ANI/ENI signals.  When TX, the
 repeater will only transmit it's programmed 5 Tone ANI/ENI
 signals.  When TRX, both of the above are active.
#
:page
#

 When any of the On states are selected, the F4 key (see
 the red boxes at the bottom of the screen) allows access
 to the 5-Tone Settings window, where the ANI/ENI 
 parameters can be programmed.  Note: The Mark and Space 
 Time parameters affect both the RX (decode) and TX 
 (encode) operation.  Incoming ID strings from other 
 radios must match the Mark and Space Time settings here 
 to be reliably decoded.

 5-Tone ID strings are (of course) always 5 characters in 
 length.  The first incoming character must be the ANI 
 Header Code character.
#
:page
#

 Alphanumeric displays for up to 48 incoming IDs can be
 programmed into the repeater.  The F6 key (see the red 
 boxes at the bottom of the screen) allows access to the
 Alpha Tag table for programming.  This table does not
 use the Header Code digit.  When an incoming (numeric) 
 ID matches an entry in this table, the programmed Alpha 
 Tag is displayed instead of the actual number.  If an 
 incoming ID is not in the table (but does have the 
 correct header character), the numeric ID is displayed.
#
:page
#

 Also see CWID ANI/ENI on the Main Programming Screen to 
 select the item that will be in use on any particular 
 channel.  CWID and ANI/ENI cannot both be active on any 
 one channel. 

 Arrow Keys: Change fields after selection.
 Space Bar:  Toggle RX Enable/TX Enable/TRX Enable/off
#
#
#
:dmarkt

                      [DTMF Mark Time]

 This is the amount of time that each tone is actually
 transmitted.  Use the number keys to program this
 value in 1 mS increments from 1 mS to 600 mS.

 Incoming ANI and ENI strings to be decoded must match
 this timing as closely as possible for reliable decode.
#
#
#
:dspacet

                     [DTMF Space Time]

 This is the amount of space time between each tone.
 Use the number keys to program this value in 1 mS
 increments from 1 mS to 600 mS.

 Incoming ANI and ENI strings to be decoded must match
 this timing as closely as possible for reliable decode.
#
#
#
:danion

                         [ANI ON]

 This selects the time at which the ANI string is
 transmitted by the repeater.  Use the Space bar to
 select TX Off (when PTT is unkeyed), TX On (when PTT
 is first keyed, plus the programmed Delay Time),Both,
 or None.  Note: The ANI is only transmitted when the
 repeater is in Base mode.
#
#
#
:danidly

                     [ANI Delay Time]

 This is the amount of time delay between PTT press
 (or release, as appropriate) and the start of the
 ANI transmission.  This is commonly known as "Front
 Porch Delay".  Use the number keys to program this
 Value in 5 mS increments from 20 mS to 1275 mS.
#
#
#
:denidly

                     [ENI Delay Time]

 This is the amount of time delay between ENI
 triggering and the start of the ENI sequence.  This 
 is commonly known as "Front Porch Delay".  Use the 
 number keys to program this value in 5 mS increments 
 from 50 mS to 12750 mS (12.75 seconds).
#
#
#
:denitx

                       [ENI TX Time]

 After the ENI sequence is triggered, this is the 
 amount of time that the transmitter will remain on.
 If a local microphone is connected to the repeater,
 any local ambient audio will be transmitted during
 this time.  Use the number keys to program this
 value in 1 second increments from 1 second to 255
 seconds.
#
#
#
:denirx

                       [ENI RX Time}

 After the ENI TX time, this is the amount of
 time that the repeater will be in normal receive
 mode.  Any on-channel traffic will be heard through
 the repeater's speaker during this period.  Use the
 number keys to program this value in 1 second
 increments from 1 second to 255 seconds.
#
#
#
:denirxd

                    [ENI RX Dead Time]

 After the ENI RX time, this is the amount of
 time that the repeater will be in receive dead
 mode.  Nothing will be heard through the repeater's 
 speaker during this period.  Use the number keys to 
 program this value in 1 second increments from 1 
 second to 255 seconds.
#
#
#
:denirp

                     [ENI Repeat Count]

 This is the number of times that the ENI sequence
 will be automatically repeated.  Use the number keys
 to program this value in increments of 1 event
 from 1 time to 255 times.
#
#
#
:danihead

                     [ANI Header Code]

 This programs the ONE leading character for all
 incoming (received) and outgoing (transmitted) ANIs.
 All incoming ANIs must have this character in the
 first position in order to be decoded.  This will 
 also be the first character in all outgoing ANIs.
 Use the number or letter keys to program any one of
 the 16 standard DTMF characters.  E=*, F=#.
#
#
#
:denihead

                     [ENI Header Code]

 This programs the ONE leading character for the
 incoming (received) and outgoing (transmitted) ENI.
 The incoming ENI must have this character in the
 first position in order to be decoded.  This will 
 also be the first character in the outgoing ENI.
 Use the number or letter keys to program any one of
 the 16 standard DTMF characters.  E=*, F=#.
#
#
#
:danicode

                        [ANI Code]

 This programs the trailing four characters of the
 repeater's outgoing ANI.  The ANI Header Code is
 always added to the beginning of this string, making
 it a five character ANI.  Use the number or letter
 keys to program any four character DTMF sequence.
 E=*, F=#.
#
#
#
:denicode

                         [ENI Code]

 This programs the trailing four characters of the
 repeater's incoming and outgoing ENI.  The ENI Header 
 Code is always added to the beginning of this string, 
 making it a five character ENI.  Use the number or 
 letter keys to program any four character DTMF 
 sequence.  E=*, F=#.
#
#
#
:5markt

                     [5 Tone Mark Time]

 This is the amount of time that each tone is actually
 transmitted.  Use the number keys to program this
 value in 1 mS increments from 1 mS to 600 mS.

 Incoming ANI and ENI strings to be decoded must match
 this timing as closely as possible for reliable decode.
#
#
#
:5spacet

                     [5 Tone Space Time]

 This is the amount of space time between each tone.
 Use the number keys to program this value in 1 mS
 increments from 1 mS to 600 mS.

 Incoming ANI and ENI strings to be decoded must match
 this timing as closely as possible for reliable decode.
#
#
#
:5anihead

                      [ANI Header Code]

 This programs the ONE leading character for all
 incoming (received) and outgoing (transmitted) ANIs.
 All incoming ANIs must have this character in the
 first position in order to be decoded.  This will 
 also be the first character in all outgoing ANIs.
 Use the number or letter keys to program any one of
 the characters for the 5 tone system in use.
#
#
#
:5enihead

                      [ENI Header Code]

 This programs the ONE leading character for the
 incoming (received) and outgoing (transmitted) ENI.
 The incoming ENI must have this character in the
 first position in order to be decoded.  This will 
 also be the first character in the outgoing ENI.
 Use the number or letter keys to program any one of
 the characters for the 5 tone system in use.
#
#
#
:5anicode

                         [ANI Code]

 This programs the trailing four characters of the
 repeater's outgoing ANI.  The ANI Header Code is
 always added to the beginning of this string, making
 it a five character ANI.  Use the number or letter
 keys to program any of the characters for the 5 tone
 system in use.
#
#
#
:5enicode

                         [ENI Code]

 This programs the trailing four characters of the
 repeater's incoming and outgoing ENI.  The ENI Header 
 Code is always added to the beginning of this string, 
 making it a five character ENI.  Use the number or 
 letter keys to program any of the characters for the
 5 tone system in use.
#
#
#
:5rptcod

                     [5 Tone Repeat Code]

 This selects the one 5 tone character that will serve
 as the repeat code character for the 5 tone system in
 use.  Use the number or letter keys to program one
 character for repeat code.
#
#
#
:5toneset

                        [5 Tone Set]

 This selects the 5 tone system (EIA, CCIR, etc.) to
 be used for signaling in this repeater.  Press the
 Space bar to scroll through the various pre-programmed
 systems.
#
:page
#

 When the "USER" set is selected, the programmer can
 edit each individual tone frequency as desired to
 customize the tone signaling system.  Use the arrow keys 
 to move the cursor.  Use the number keys to enter a new
 tone frequency for any character(s).
#
#
#
:edit_enviro
              [Hardware Environment Settings]

 CAUTION: Do not change Hardware Environment Parameters 
 unless you are certain that the transceiver hardware is
 suitable for the new value.

 Editing these parameters requires that this program be
 started with the -f switch on the command line.  
 Otherwise the settings can only be viewed.

 Use the arrow keys to select the parameter to edit.  Then 
 Use the Space bar or Backspace key to toggle among all 
 Possible settings for this parameter.

 When all parameters are set as desired, press the Escape
 key to return to the Common Data Editor.
#
#
#
:edit_common

               [Edit Common Data Parameters]

 CAUTION: Do not change Common Parameters unless you are
 certain that the transceiver hardware is suitable for 
 the new value.

 Editing these parameters requires that this program be
 started with the -f switch on the command line. Otherwise
 the settings can only be viewed.

 Use the Arrow Keys to select the parameter to edit. Then use
 the Space Bar or Backspace Key to toggle among all possible
 settings for that parameter. When all parameters are set as
 desired, press the Esc Key to return to the Channel Editor.
#
#
#
:txpwradj

                      [TX Power Adjust]

 This window allows the programmer to adjust the 
 transmitter high power output on a per-channel basis.  

 This does not provide real-time adjustments.  Upload (F5)
 the program from the repeater.  Bring up the TX Power 
 Adjust window.  Use the Arrow keys to select the channel 
 to adjust.  Use the Space bar to increment, or the 
 Backspace key to decrement, the transmitter power setting 
 to the desired numeric level.  Escape back to the Main 
 Programming Screen and download (F6) the program back to 
 the repeater.  Check the transmitter output power.  If 
 further adjustment is required, repeat the above steps.
#
:page
#
 The FRB-4 Tuning I/F Box may be used for this procedure
 if desired, but it is not required.  It will save you
 considerable time because you will not have to swap
 cables back and forth.

 When using the VXD-40xx internal duplexer, the transmit
 power must be reduced to 40 watts.  A power setting of 
 -28(E4h) will usually reduce the output to 40 watts.  
 This must be adjusted and verified BEFORE the duplexer 
 is installed. 

 Programmed power levels will be saved to disk when the
 repeater's program is saved (F4 at the Main Programming
 Screen).
#
#
#
:alphatag

                      [ANI Alpha Tag]

 The ANI readout can be programmed to "alias" any
 incoming ANI number sequence and display instead any
 alphanumeric name up to 8 characters.  Enter the
 4 digit ANI code (this is the last 4 digits...the first
 digit must always be the ANI Header Code digit) into
 the left side of the table, then enter the desired
 alphanumeric name for display on the right side of
 the table.  Up to 48 entries can be made.

 If an ANI number is received that does not have a
 programmed name, the actual decoded DTMF or 5 tone
 digits will be displayed.
#
#
#
:iddottim

                        [ID Dot Time]

 This sets the speed of the Morse station ID.  A dot
 time of 50 mS is equivalent to a Morse speed of
 approximately 25 words per minute.
#
#
#
:idintvtim

                     [ID Interval Timer]

 This sets the time interval between station IDs.
 When this time expires, the identifier will wait 
 until the current user unkeys his radio before 
 sending the ID.  ID will not be sent if there has
 been no radio activity since the last ID was sent.
#
#
#
:idtonfrq
 
                     [ID Tone Frequency]

 This selects the tone frequency ("pitch") for the
 Identifier.
#
#
#
:idcwid

                         [CW-ID]

 This is the ID string that will be sent in Morse
 code.  Use the letter and number keys to enter up
 to 16 characters for transmission.  The Space bar
 will enter a word space (Dot Time x 3) between
 characters if desired.
#
#
#
:envsqwn

                   [Squelch W/N Adjust]

 This parameter adjusts the difference in the squelch
 noise level relationship between channels programmed
 in Wide mode and those programmed in Narrow mode. 

 In most cases, the factory default setting should
 not be changed.

 Use the number keys to type numeric entries, or
 use the Space bar and Backspace keys to increment
 or decrement the setting.  Download the changed
 data to the repeater to effect the change.
#
#
#
:envsqhy

                    [Squelch Hysteresis]

 This parameter adjusts the width of the squelch
 hysteresis, from point of opening to point of
 closing.  

 Use the number keys to type numeric entries, or
 use the Space bar and Backspace keys to increment
 or decrement the setting.  Download the changed
 data to the repeater to effect the change.
#
#
#
:envnsqth

                   [NSQ Threshold Level]

 This parameter adjusts the master repeat squelch
 threshold level.  This item sets the threshold for
 the "Prpgm" squelch level as seen on the main 
 programming screen.  

 Use the number keys to type numeric entries, or
 use the Space bar and Backspace keys to increment
 or decrement the setting.  Download the changed
 data to the repeater to effect the change.

 Info:  From this half of the Environment screen, 
 press the Tab key to move the cursor to the other 
 half of the screen.
#
#
#
:envrxfrq

                      [RX Freq (MHz)]

 This half of the screen allows adjustment of 
 various parameters that are linked to the 
 repeater's receive frequency.  Think of these 
 adjustments as being similar to those found
 on a graphic equalizer.  For example, the setting
 of the TX Power parameter under the Lowest (150.0
 MHz) column will have effect on operating 
 frequencies at and near 150 MHz.  It will have no
 effect on the same parameter when the repeater is
 operating at 170 MHz.
#
:page
#

 Ordinarily, these frequency band settings should
 not be changed, as they do affect the repeater's
 performance across it's entire frequency range.
 However, if this repeater is to be operated only
 on one set RX/TX frequency (pair) that will NEVER 
 be changed throughout it's operating life, and if
 the technician desires a very high level of
 alignment response only at one particular
 RX/TX frequency (pair), the desired exact frequency 
 may be input here in the column most closely
 associated with that frequency.  For example, if
 the exact frequency is 153.530 MHz, enter 153.5 in
 the Lowest (150.0) column header.  This entry is
 limited to a resolution of 100 kHz.  
#
#
#
:envsqllvl

                       [SQL Level]

 This item sets the relative repeat squelch 
 threshold level for each frequency range.  This
 setting can also be offset by the "User" squelch
 setting on the main programming screen on a per-
 channel basis.

 Use the number keys to type numeric entries, or
 use the Space bar and Backspace keys to increment
 or decrement the setting.  Download the changed
 data to the repeater to effect the change.
#
#
#
:envrxt

                        [RX Tune]

 This item tunes the receiver front end for each
 frequency range.  Lower numbers generally provide
 better sensitivity for lower frequencies, and higher
 numbers generally provide better sensitivity for
 higher frequencies.

 Use the number keys to type numeric entries, or
 use the Space bar and Backspace keys to increment
 or decrement the setting.  Download the changed
 data to the repeater to effect the change.
#
#
#
:envtxfreq

                      [TX Freq (MHz)]

 This half of the screen allows adjustment of 
 various parameters that are linked to the 
 repeater's transmit frequency.  Think of these 
 adjustments as being similar to those found
 on a graphic equalizer.  For example, the setting
 of the TX Power parameter under the Lowest (150.0
 MHz) column will have effect on operating 
 frequencies at and near 150 MHz.  It will have no
 effect on the same parameter when the repeater is
 operating at 170 MHz.
#
:page
#

 Ordinarily, these frequency band settings should
 not be changed, as they do affect the repeater's
 performance across it's entire frequency range.
 However, if this repeater is to be operated only
 on one set RX/TX frequency (pair) that will NEVER 
 be changed throughout it's operating life, and if
 the technician desires a very high level of
 alignment response only at one particular
 RX/TX frequency (pair), the desired exact frequency 
 may be input here in the column most closely
 associated with that frequency.  For example, if
 the exact frequency is 153.530 MHz, enter 153.5 in
 the Lowest (150.0) column header.  This entry is
 limited to a resolution of 100 kHz.   
#
#
#
:envtxpwr

                     [TX Pwr Hi / Lo]

 These items adjust the transmitter's master high and 
 low power output.  These settings can then be offset
 (plus or minus) on a per-channel basis in the TXP 
 Adj table.  It is best to set all the offsets in 
 the TXP Adj table to 0 before aligning TX power here.

 Use the number keys to type numeric entries, or
 use the Space bar and Backspace keys to increment
 or decrement the setting.  Download the changed
 data to the repeater to effect the change.
#
#
#
:envmaxd

                      [Max Dev W / N]

 These items adjust the transmitter's overall
 maximum total deviation.  Inject a 1kHz audio
 test tone at 77mV into the microphone jack
 (the FRB-4 Interface Box is very helpful for this),
 key the transmitter, and align the deviation to the 
 desired level.

 Hint:  If transmitting CTCSS or DCS, it is wise
 to align these sub-audio deviation levels BEFORE
 aligning the Maximum Deviation.
#
:page
#

 Use the number keys to type numeric entries, or
 use the Space bar and Backspace keys to increment
 or decrement the setting.  Download the changed
 data to the repeater to effect the change.
#
#
#
:envctcd

                   [CTC (CTCSS) Dev W / N]

 These items adjust the transmitter's CTCSS
 deviation.  Be sure that the selected channel is
 programmed to transmit CTCSS.  Key the transmitter 
 and align the deviation to the desired level.

 Use the number keys to type numeric entries, or
 use the Space bar and Backspace keys to increment
 or decrement the setting.  Download the changed
 data to the repeater to effect the change.
#
#
#
:envdcsd

                      [DCS Dev W / N]

 These items adjust the transmitter's DCS
 deviation.  Be sure that the selected channel is
 programmed to transmit DCS.  Key the transmitter
 and align the deviation to the desired level.

 Use the number keys to type numeric entries, or
 use the Space bar and Backspace keys to increment
 or decrement the setting.  Download the changed
 data to the repeater to effect the change.
#
:file_select
                  [File Selection Window]

 Select a filename (lower-case letters) or subdirectory
 (upper-case, in angle brackets <>) using the Arrow Keys, and
 then the Enter Key to accept your selection. If you selected
 a subdirectory, the window contents will be replaced with
 that subdirectory's contents, from which you can select
 again. You can also delete a file or subdirectory with [F4].

 You can also enter a new drive letter or filespec directly.
 Pressing alphanumeric keys activates the filespec line
 editor along the bottom of the window. You can use wildcards
 ("*" or "?") to display only certain files (eg.,
 B:\CHDATA\*.CH and Enter displays only files with the "CH"
 extension in the CHDATA subdirectory on drive B). Tab
 returns you to the upper half of the selection window.
#
:page
#
 (at any time in Selection Window)
 Enter Key:  Accept selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel operation and return to Channel Editor.
 Tab Key:    Switch between Selection Window and line editor.

 (for selection in upper part of window)
 Arrow Keys: Select filename/subdirectory.
 Home/End:   Jump to first/last selection on screen.
 [F4] Key:   Delete current file selection from disk.
 PageDown:   Jump down one page (for large directories).
 PageUp:     Jump up one page (for large directories).

 (for filespec editor at bottom of window)
 Delete:     Delete character under cursor.
 Backspace:  Delete character to left.
 Insert:     Toggle insert/overwrite modes.
#
#
#
:path_edit
                   [File Selection Window]

 Select a filename (lower-case letters) or subdirectory
 (upper-case, in angle brackets <>) using the Arrow Keys, and
 then the Enter Key to accept your selection. If you selected
 a subdirectory, the window contents will be replaced with
 that subdirectory's contents, from which you can select
 again.  If you select a file, you can delete it with [F4].

 You can also enter a new drive letter or filespec directly.
 Pressing alphanumeric keys activates the filespec line
 editor along the bottom of the window. You can use wildcards
 ("*" or "?") to display only certain files (eg.,
 B:\CHDATA\*.CH and Enter displays only files with the "CH"
 extension in the CHDATA subdirectory on drive B). Tab 
 returns you to the upper half of the selection window.
#
:page
#
 (at any time in Selection Window)
 Enter Key:  Accept selection.
 Esc Key:    Cancel operation and return to Channel Editor.
 Tab Key:    Switch between Selection Window and line editor.

 (for selection in upper part of window)
 Arrow Keys: Select filename/subdirectory.
 Home/End:   Jump to first/last selection on screen.
 [F4] Key:   Delete current file selection from disk.
 PageDown:   Jump down one page (for large directories).
 PageUp:     Jump up one page (for large directories).

 (for filespec editor at bottom of window)
 Delete:     Delete character under cursor.
 Backspace:  Delete character to left.
 Insert:     Toggle insert/overwrite modes.
#
#
#
:read_rom

     [Transfer data between PC and Transceiver's EEPROM]

 The Space Bar is used to proceed with the transfer operation
 after ensuring the hardware is set up for the transfer. To
 cancel and return to the Channel Editor, press the Esc Key
 instead.

 The status of the transfer operation will be displayed when
 finished (a few seconds after pressing the Space Bar).

 NOTE! Press the Esc Key if the system appears to hang. Then
 make sure the power indicator on the interface box is on,
 and check the cables for a connection problem.
#
#
#
:write_rom

    [Transfer data between PC and Transceiver's EEPROM]

 The Space Bar is used to proceed with the transfer operation
 after ensuring the hardware is set up for the transfer. To
 cancel and return to the Channel Editor, press the Esc Key
 instead.

 The status of the transfer operation will be displayed when
 finished (a few seconds after pressing the Space Bar).

 NOTE! Press the Esc Key if the system appears to hang. Then
 make sure the power indicator on the interface box is on,
 and check the cables for a connection problem.
#
#
#
:make_dir

          [Make a new subdirectory on current Path]

 Input the name of the new subdirectory (up to 8 characters),
 then press Enter. Press Esc or F8 to cancel if you change
 your mind.

 Backspace: Delete character to left of cursor
 Delete:    Delete character above cursor
 Insert:    Toggle Insert/Overwrite mode (Insert is default,
            Overwrite has block cursor)
#
#
#
:remove_dir
       [Remove the named (or pointed-to) subdirectory]

 Only empty child directories from the current subdirectory
 can be removed. In the file list, the inverse box can be
 positioned with the arrows to select a subdirectory to
 remove, by pressing F4. If the file list selection is not
 a subdirectory, a box will appear in which you can enter
 the name of the subdirectory to be removed. This name must
 be the same as one of the subdirectories displayed in the
 file list, and any files in that subdirectory must have
 already been deleted.

 Backspace: Delete character to left of cursor
 Delete:    Delete character above cursor
 Insert:    Toggle Insert/Overwrite mode (Insert is default,
               Overwrite has block cursor)
#
#

